<nav class="screen-nav" aria-label="Workbook"><a href="/try/">First-session guide</a> · <a href="/workbooks/spectrum-basic-first-session/teacher.pdf">Download this edition as PDF</a> · <a href="/try/workbook/learner/">Learner booklet</a></nav><article class="workbook-root"><section class="sheet"><h1>Sharing a first BASIC session</h1><p class="edition">Teacher notes · Companion to learner workbook v0.1 · 17 October 2026</p><p class="lead">Help learners make a prediction, test one deliberate change and explain the result. These notes contain hints and answers; keep them separate from the pupil booklet.</p><h2>Prepare on the actual computers</h2><ol><li>Open <a href="https://code198x.com/try/spectrum-basic/">code198x.com/try/spectrum-basic/</a> and follow its linked lesson to <strong>Run an editable listing</strong>.</li><li>Check editing, <strong>Run this code</strong>, <strong>Back to the listing</strong>, <strong>Listing options → Download tape</strong> and reopening a saved text listing after reloading the lesson. Test the school network and download restrictions before the session.</li><li>Print one learner booklet per learner or pair, A4 at actual size. It works in black and white. Provide paper or another way to record explanations.</li><li>Try larger text and keyboard controls on the devices you will use. Ask what helps each learner read, type or compare results.</li></ol><p>No account, original Spectrum or local installation is needed for this activity. A browser that can run the embedded tools and an internet connection are needed for live testing. Paper tracing remains available if those are blocked.</p><h3>What to look for</h3><ul><li>A prediction before running.</li><li>A deliberate edit to one quoted message, with the other instructions preserved.</li><li>An explanation pointing to the instruction responsible.</li><li>A kept copy, checked by reopening if the tools are available.</li></ul><h3>Organise the group</h3><p>Pair an editor/operator with a predictor/checker and swap roles after the first change. Invite experienced learners to explore line ordering without supplying everyone else’s answers. Use the first changed greeting as a stopping point; extend only when useful. No fixed completion time is promised.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>1 / 3</span></footer></section><section class="sheet"><h2>Hints and expected results</h2><p>The maintained starting listing is:</p><pre><code>  10 PRINT &quot;Welcome&quot;
  15 PRINT &quot;to you&quot;
  20 PRINT &quot;from the Spectrum&quot;</code></pre><p>With no changes, its output is:</p><pre>Welcome
to you
from the Spectrum</pre><p>Changing only the quoted message in line 10 changes only the first output line. The learner’s chosen words need not match the example on the booklet’s first page.</p><h3>Offer a hint before the answer</h3><p>Ask “Which instruction contains those words?” or “What stayed the same?” Point to one line if needed. Keep quotation marks and the remaining instructions intact.</p><h3>Optional renumbering experiment</h3><p>In the text listing, replacing line number 15 with 25 produces running order 10, 20, 25. The output is the learner’s greeting, then <code>from the Spectrum</code>, then <code>to you</code>.</p><p>This straight-line program follows increasing line numbers, not the order in which the lines were edited. Do not generalise that statement to programs with jumps or other control flow.</p><p>Direct Spectrum entry behaves differently: entering <code>25 PRINT "to you"</code> adds line 25; it does not remove line 15. In a direct session, a line number entered alone deletes that stored line. The workbook uses the complete text editor so learners change the existing source line.</p><h3>When something differs</h3><ul><li><strong>Syntax error:</strong> check quotation marks and spelling against the listing. Treat the error location as a clue.</li><li><strong>Old output:</strong> confirm that the edited listing was run and the expected greeting was changed.</li><li><strong>Unexpected order or extra message:</strong> inspect all stored line numbers and distinguish direct keyboard entry from replacing the text listing.</li></ul><p>Ask learners to retain both their prediction and observation. A changed explanation after an unexpected result is useful evidence of understanding.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>2 / 3</span></footer></section><section class="sheet"><h2>Save, follow up and reuse</h2><p>Keep the complete readable listing in a saved plain text file. Reload the lesson, replace the whole editable listing with the saved source, then choose <strong>Run this code</strong>. Check that the learner’s chosen words reappear. This establishes recovery of their kept source, not merely a successful save report.</p><p><strong>Listing options → Download tape</strong> also retains the edited listing as a <code>.tap</code> program for an emulator with tape-loading controls. The current editable browser panel has no tape file picker; its recovery path is to paste saved source into the listing.</p><p>Typing BASIC <code>SAVE</code> into this browser Spectrum is a different workflow. No recorder is attached to retain that signal. Use the listing controls to download a tape image.</p><h3>A useful closing conversation</h3><ul><li>“Show me the instruction you changed.”</li><li>“What made that instruction different from its result?”</li><li>“How would you check a kept program tomorrow?”</li></ul><p>Accept explanations in words, drawings or code. A copied program that runs is not, by itself, evidence that its author understands it. Offer pauses and help, and avoid turning the activity into a scored gate.</p><h3>Where to go next</h3><p><a href="https://code198x.com/try/spectrum-basic/">BASIC activity and next lesson</a> · <a href="https://code198x.com/try/parents-and-teachers/">First-session companion</a> · <a href="https://code198x.com/teaching/">Broader teaching guide</a></p><p>Assembly remains an independent option at <a href="https://code198x.com/try/spectrum-assembly/">code198x.com/try/spectrum-assembly/</a>. This BASIC booklet is not a prerequisite.</p><h3>Source and reuse</h3><p class="credits">Adapted from Code Like It's 198x, <em>Make the Spectrum answer</em>, <em>Make a greeting with Spectrum BASIC</em> and <em>Sharing a first Code198x session</em>. This adaptation adds paper response space, paired activity prompts and separate expected results. Version 0.1, 17 October 2026.</p><p class="credits">Original and adapted prose: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International</a>. Credit Code Like It's 198x, link the source and licence, and indicate further changes. Code samples: MIT, with the accompanying <a href="/workbooks/spectrum-basic-first-session/CODE-LICENSE.txt">code licence notice</a>. This prose licence does not cover third-party assets or project marks.</p><p class="credits">The sample comes from <code>meet-basic/opening/unit-01/steps/step-04.bas</code>. The accompanying <a href="/workbooks/spectrum-basic-first-session/sources.json">source record</a> gives the full repository path, revision and hash. Sinclair behaviour is described in Steven Vickers, edited by Robin Bradbeer, <em>ZX Spectrum BASIC Programming</em>, second edition (Sinclair Research, 1983), chapter 2 and introductory editing notes, cited in the online lesson.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>3 / 3</span></footer></section></article>
